.SH PORTABILITY
Applications employing
.I \%ncurses
extensions should condition their use on the visibility of the
.B \%NCURSES_VERSION
preprocessor macro.
.PP
X/Open Curses Issue\ 4 describes these functions.
It specifies no error conditions for them.
.PP
.I \%ncurses
satisfies X/Open Curses's minimum maximums for
.I \%COLORS
and
.IR \%COLOR_PAIRS "."
.PP
.I \%ncurses
does not refresh the screen if
.B \%init_pair
is used to no effect on an existing color pair.
.PP
X/Open Curses does not specify a limit for the number of color indices
and color pair identifiers a terminal can support.
However,
in its use of
.I short
for the parameters,
it carries over SVr4's implementation detail for the compiled
.I \%term\%info
database,
which uses signed 16-bit numbers.
.I \%ncurses
provides extended versions of the functions using
.I int
parameters,
allowing applications to use larger index and pair identifiers.
.PP
SVr4
.I curses
returns
.I ERR
from
.I \%pair_content
if its
.I pair
argument
was not initialized using
.IR \%init_pairs ,
and from
.I \%color_content
if the terminal does not support changing colors.
.I \%ncurses
does neither.
